<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Daigo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/884">884</a>–<a href="/years/930">930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Daigo-tennō, Minamoto no Korezane, Atsuhito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Daigo was the 60th emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> according to the traditional order of succession, reigning from 897 to 930. The eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-uda">Emperor Uda</a>, he was born a subject named Minamoto no Korezane, because his grandfather Emperor Kōkō had demoted his sons from the imperial ranks, and became an imperial prince only in 887. The chief feature of his 34-year reign was that he ruled by himself without a Fujiwara regent, though he was part Fujiwara himself; in his reign Ki no Tsurayuki presented the Kokin Wakashū. Falling ill in 930, he abdicated, became a monk and died, and was buried at Daigo-ji, which gave him his posthumous name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-daigo.jpg" alt="Emperor Daigo" width="330" height="660" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Daigo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Daigo was born on 6 February 884, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-uda">Emperor Uda</a>. His mother was Fujiwara no Taneko, daughter of the minister Fujiwara no Takafuji. She died before his accession, and he was raised by another consort of Uda, Fujiwara no Onshi, daughter of the former kampaku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune">Fujiwara no Mototsune</a>. His grandfather Emperor Kōkō had demoted his own sons to subjects, giving them the family name Minamoto, to reduce state expenses and their political influence. For that reason Daigo was named Minamoto no Korezane until 887, and his personal name was later changed to Atsuhito. Uda abdicated in 897, and Daigo formally acceded to the throne on 14 August 897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The era name was changed in 898 to mark the beginning of the reign. Its notable feature was that Daigo governed himself, without a regency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, although he was himself partly Fujiwara. In 901 the incident of Sugawara Michizane developed, but its details cannot be known because Daigo ordered the diaries and records of the period burned. In 906 Ki no Tsurayuki presented to the emperor the compilation of the Kokin Wakashū, a collection of waka poetry. Floods devastated the country in 929, and many people died.</p><p>On 21 July 930 lightning struck the Imperial Palace during a great storm and killed Senior Counselor Fujiwara no Kiyotsura and Taira no Mareyo among others. The deaths were construed as the revenge of the unsettled spirit of the late Sugawara Michizane. Daigo had 21 empresses, consorts and concubines and 36 children, and two of his sons later became emperors, Suzaku and Murakami. He also ordered the building of several halls at Daigo-ji, such as the Yakushi hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 16 October 930 Daigo fell ill and, fearing that he would not survive, abdicated, and his son received the succession. In the early hours of 23 October he entered the Buddhist priesthood, taking the name Hō-kongō, and shortly afterwards he died, aged 46. He was buried in the precincts of Daigo-ji, which gave him his posthumous name. The site of his grave is known, and the Imperial Household Agency designates it as his mausoleum in Fushimi-ku, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Daigo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kanmu and his successors down to Emperor Saga governed in person, and <b>Emperor Daigo</b> ruled directly from 897 to 930.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35,135.77&amp;year=913" aria-label="Heian-kyō on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emperor Daigo"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4013 4511h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4013 4511h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35,135.77&amp;year=913">What was here in 913 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
